Mental Health Care Plan Creation/Review
General Practice Other
Formal mental health diagnosis, Standardized psychometric assessment, Formal risk evaluation, Referral for psychological therapy

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-026
Reason for Visit: Acute psychiatric decompensation and severe panic attack requiring immediate psychological stabilization and formulation of a formal management plan.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute exacerbation of severe anxiety and depressive symptoms culminating in a profound panic attack tonight, rendering the patient unable to function or ensure basic self-care. Urgent face-to-face assessment was required to conduct an immediate suicide risk assessment, provide pharmacological and psychological de-escalation, and formulate a formal Mental Health Treatment Plan to secure urgent subsidized psychological therapy. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period posed an unacceptable risk of escalating self-harm or an unwarranted emergency department presentation.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I haven't slept in three days, I can't stop crying, and tonight my chest got so tight I thought I was dying."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a diagnosis of a formalized mental health condition, specifically severe generalized anxiety disorder with secondary depressive features. The patient reports persistent psychological symptoms significantly impacting daily functioning for greater than two weeks, culminating in tonight's acute panic episode. A detailed assessment of the patient's current psychological, social, and occupational context reveals overwhelming workplace stress and recent marital conflict. The patient describes a constant sense of dread, severe insomnia, and an inability to concentrate. A formal risk assessment evaluating current suicidal ideation, self-harm, or risk to others indicates passive death wishes ("I just want to go to sleep and not wake up"), but denies any formulated plan, intent, or access to lethal means.
Functional Status: Currently highly impaired by acute panic and exhaustion; usually independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute situational distress; partner is highly supportive and acting as a protective factor.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 140/90 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Tearful, hyperventilating, wringing hands, poor eye contact.
Physical Exam: Psychiatric: Affect is highly anxious and depressed. Speech is rapid but coherent. Thought processes are logical but entirely fixated on overwhelming stressors. No evidence of psychosis, delusions, or hallucinations. The clinical presentation required the administration of standardized psychometric questionnaires, specifically the K10, which yielded a score of 38 (indicating severe psychological distress). The assessment involves collaborative goal setting identifying specific therapeutic targets (sleep restoration and panic management), and a referral for subsidized psychological therapies including cognitive behavioural therapy. Expected investigation results include elevated scores on validated psychometric screening tools reflecting significant clinical distress.
Environmental Observations: Safe, quiet home environment; no obvious environmental risks or access to weapons.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular psychotropic medications; previously trialed Sertraline 5 years ago with poor tolerance.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Mental Health Treatment Plan Creation/Review (Acute Exacerbation of GAD) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Tachycardia and chest tightness resolving completely with structured breathing exercises and verbal de-escalation; typical presentation of a panic attack.

Bipolar Affective Disorder (Manic Phase) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of elevated mood, grandiosity, or pressured speech; insomnia is driven by worry, not a decreased need for sleep.

Substance-Induced Anxiety Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Patient denies any recent use of illicit substances, excessive caffeine, or sudden alcohol withdrawal.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Due to passive SI)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced a severe, acute panic attack superimposed on a background of worsening generalized anxiety and depression. Telephone triage correctly identified the necessity for immediate psychiatric evaluation to rule out active suicidality and physical pathology causing the chest pain. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to provide acute crisis intervention, perform a formal, standardized mental health assessment (K10), and legally generate a Mental Health Treatment Plan to unlock immediate subsidized access to a clinical psychologist. Deferring this intervention until the next in-hours period would have left the patient in a state of severe, unmanageable distress and elevated the risk of impulsive self-harm overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Conducted extensive verbal de-escalation and grounding exercises. Completed a formal Mental Health Treatment Plan (MBS Item 2715) and generated a referral to a local clinical psychologist. Prescribed Diazepam 2mg (dispensed 3 tablets only) strictly to break the acute cycle of severe insomnia tonight. Discussed initiating a daily SSRI (Escitalopram) to be formalized at the follow-up appointment.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the acute physical symptoms of panic, immediately initiate the "4-7-8" breathing technique (inhale for 4 seconds, hold for 7, exhale for 8) at the very first physical sign of a racing heart. Practice this technique for two minutes, twice daily, even when you feel calm. By practicing the technique when your baseline anxiety is low, you build a strong, automatic physiological response that your brain can easily access during a high-stress panic spike.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 72 hours to assess the response to the initial intervention, review the K10 score, and formalize the ongoing pharmacological plan.

